1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,590
The universe is thirteen
point eight billion years old.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,480
And humans appeared here
only a tiny fraction

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,950
of a second ago, on the cosmic timescale.

4
00:00:10,375 --> 00:00:12,305
So here's the famous question.

5
00:00:13,138 --> 00:00:14,338
Where is everybody?

6
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:21,721
If intelligent life is common, shouldn't
somebody have colonised the galaxy by now?

7
00:00:22,151 --> 00:00:23,901
Shouldn't we have seen something?

8
00:00:24,652 --> 00:00:27,702
A signal. A spacecraft. A
megastructure. Anything.

9
00:00:28,588 --> 00:00:31,558
This is the basic idea
behind the Fermi paradox.

10
00:00:31,888 --> 00:00:34,638
And it sounds like a very simple argument.

11
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:36,734
So...

12
00:00:37,145 --> 00:00:38,415
where are the aliens?

13
00:00:39,502 --> 00:00:40,702
But there's a problem.

14
00:00:41,502 --> 00:00:44,912
The Fermi paradox isn't really a
conflict between two observations.

15
00:00:46,142 --> 00:00:47,512
We observe one thing.

16
00:00:48,042 --> 00:00:50,252
We haven't detected
extraterrestrial civilisation.

17
00:00:51,849 --> 00:00:54,839
And then we combine it with a
large collection of assumptions.

18
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:58,959
They should be common. They
should become intelligent.

19
00:01:00,009 --> 00:01:01,879
They should develop advanced technology.

20
00:01:02,853 --> 00:01:05,363
They should survive. They
should want to expand.

21
00:01:06,293 --> 00:01:08,683
And they should leave traces
that we can recognise.

22
00:01:09,735 --> 00:01:14,585
If even one of those assumptions is wrong,
the paradox becomes much less paradoxical.

23
00:01:15,315 --> 00:01:16,785
So let's take them apart.

24
00:01:19,725 --> 00:01:23,855
The simplest possibility is that
technological intelligence is rare.

25
00:01:24,905 --> 00:01:26,575
Maybe life itself is common.

26
00:01:27,311 --> 00:01:30,311
But perhaps getting from a
primitive cell to a civilisation

27
00:01:30,391 --> 00:01:35,361
capable of building radio telescopes
is extraordinarily unlikely.

28
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:38,930
And we have one inconvenient problem here.

29
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:43,710
We have exactly one confirmed example
of a technological civilisation.

30
00:01:45,105 --> 00:01:46,305
Us. That's it.

31
00:01:46,886 --> 00:01:51,526
We don't have ten planets with intelligent
life. We don't have a thousand.

32
00:01:51,606 --> 00:01:52,806
We have one.

33
00:01:53,192 --> 00:01:54,812
So when someone says this.

34
00:01:54,892 --> 00:01:58,042
"Intelligent life probably
evolves like this..."

35
00:01:58,152 --> 00:02:00,382
there is a rather obvious question.

36
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:02,451
Based on what?

37
00:02:04,331 --> 00:02:08,001
Earth could be typical. Or
Earth could be a freak accident.

38
00:02:08,911 --> 00:02:11,001
We simply don't have enough data to know.

39
00:02:11,769 --> 00:02:14,279
But let's assume civilisations are common.

40
00:02:15,049 --> 00:02:17,399
Would that mean the galaxy
should be full of aliens?

41
00:02:18,438 --> 00:02:22,588
Not at all. Because intelligence
isn't the same thing as expansion.

42
00:02:23,549 --> 00:02:27,999
Imagine a civilisation with
abundant energy, advanced computers,

43
00:02:28,469 --> 00:02:31,619
and virtual reality indistinguishable
from the real thing.

44
00:02:32,392 --> 00:02:34,142
And then somebody says this.

45
00:02:34,632 --> 00:02:36,762
"We should send people to another star."

46
00:02:37,546 --> 00:02:39,036
The trip takes centuries.

47
00:02:39,786 --> 00:02:42,056
It costs enormous amounts of energy.

48
00:02:42,906 --> 00:02:45,296
The destination may
contain nothing useful.

49
00:02:46,158 --> 00:02:51,568
And even if it does, there is no obvious
reason why anyone has to go there. Why?

50
00:02:52,443 --> 00:02:57,253
We assume that an advanced civilisation
will automatically become expansionist.

51
00:02:58,023 --> 00:02:59,673
But that's a very human assumption.

52
00:03:00,605 --> 00:03:02,375
Humans have always expanded.

53
00:03:03,265 --> 00:03:06,675
We spread across continents.
We crossed oceans.

54
00:03:07,665 --> 00:03:11,695
So perhaps we assume every civilisation
will do the same on a larger scale.

55
00:03:12,483 --> 00:03:13,993
But maybe that's wrong.

56
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:17,754
If your civilisation
lives inside computers,

57
00:03:18,124 --> 00:03:20,364
why travel hundreds of light-years?

58
00:03:20,784 --> 00:03:25,154
Why build cities on another planet when
you can build another universe at home?

59
00:03:25,882 --> 00:03:31,212
The classic Fermi argument needs something
much stronger than this. "Aliens exist."

60
00:03:32,188 --> 00:03:35,818
It needs this. "Aliens exist,
and they want to expand."

61
00:03:36,568 --> 00:03:38,098
Those are very different claims.

62
00:03:40,672 --> 00:03:42,462
There's a darker possibility.

63
00:03:43,352 --> 00:03:45,212
Perhaps some step on
the way from matter to

64
00:03:45,292 --> 00:03:48,262
a civilisation is almost
impossible to pass.

65
00:03:49,132 --> 00:03:51,002
It's often called the Great Filter.

66
00:03:52,183 --> 00:03:56,153
Maybe technological civilisations
almost always destroy themselves.

67
00:03:57,231 --> 00:03:59,781
But we have no evidence
that this is what happens.

68
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:05,121
The Great Filter is a possible
explanation. It is not an observation.

69
00:04:07,851 --> 00:04:11,381
There's another possibility that's
almost embarrassingly simple.

70
00:04:12,211 --> 00:04:13,741
Maybe we're searching badly.

71
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:19,120
We talk about SETI as though humanity had
searched the universe and found nothing.

72
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:21,360
But that's not what happened.

73
00:04:22,148 --> 00:04:25,518
We've searched tiny fractions
of the possible parameter space.

74
00:04:26,568 --> 00:04:28,588
We don't know what frequency
an alien civilisation

75
00:04:28,668 --> 00:04:32,098
would use, or whether it
would transmit at all.

76
00:04:32,785 --> 00:04:35,335
We don't even know what
technology it would use.

77
00:04:36,085 --> 00:04:39,395
For most of human history,
we didn't know radio existed.

78
00:04:40,285 --> 00:04:43,995
So imagine giving a radio
telescope to an ancient Roman.

79
00:04:44,705 --> 00:04:46,235
He points it at the sky.

80
00:04:47,065 --> 00:04:48,815
It detects thousands of signals.

81
00:04:49,772 --> 00:04:51,922
What does he conclude? Probably nothing.

82
00:04:52,876 --> 00:04:56,266
The signals aren't part of his
conceptual model of the universe.

83
00:04:57,175 --> 00:04:59,895
We could stare directly
at an alien civilisation's

84
00:05:00,195 --> 00:05:04,545
technological activity and have
no idea what we're looking at.

85
00:05:05,207 --> 00:05:09,217
The absence of a signal isn't
the absence of a civilisation.

86
00:05:09,847 --> 00:05:12,457
It's the absence of a signal
we know how to recognise.

87
00:05:15,417 --> 00:05:18,407
And even if they are out
there, the galaxy is enormous.

88
00:05:19,417 --> 00:05:22,727
The Milky Way is roughly a hundred
thousand light-years across.

89
00:05:23,579 --> 00:05:27,349
A civilisation could exist
somewhere in the galaxy right now...

90
00:05:28,048 --> 00:05:30,918
and its light wouldn't reach
us for thousands of years.

91
00:05:31,707 --> 00:05:34,467
There could be civilisations
that existed millions of

92
00:05:34,547 --> 00:05:38,597
years ago, and civilisations
that haven't even evolved yet.

93
00:05:39,397 --> 00:05:41,207
The galaxy isn't one moment.

94
00:05:41,877 --> 00:05:43,987
It's a huge four-dimensional history.

95
00:05:44,957 --> 00:05:47,127
We aren't looking at the galaxy as it is.

96
00:05:47,777 --> 00:05:50,927
We're looking at different parts of
it at different points in their past.

97
00:05:53,717 --> 00:05:57,427
And when we imagine aliens, we
imagine something remarkably human.

98
00:05:58,237 --> 00:06:01,327
They build machines. They
communicate. They colonise.

99
00:06:02,649 --> 00:06:03,849
But why?

100
00:06:05,464 --> 00:06:07,704
Perhaps technological
advancement eventually

101
00:06:07,784 --> 00:06:10,174
makes a civilisation less visible.

102
00:06:11,144 --> 00:06:14,954
Perhaps the loudest phase of a
civilisation is also its shortest.

103
00:06:16,015 --> 00:06:18,595
Searching for noisy civilisations
could be like searching

104
00:06:18,675 --> 00:06:22,125
for teenagers by looking
only for abandoned toys.

105
00:06:25,305 --> 00:06:27,855
Now we get to one of
the most famous ideas.

106
00:06:28,772 --> 00:06:29,972
The Dark Forest.

107
00:06:32,521 --> 00:06:34,911
Imagine the galaxy as a dark forest.

108
00:06:35,801 --> 00:06:38,971
Every civilisation is a
hunter hiding among the trees.

109
00:06:39,893 --> 00:06:41,703
You don't know who else is there.

110
00:06:42,253 --> 00:06:43,883
You don't know their intentions.

111
00:06:44,813 --> 00:06:47,143
And you don't know whether
they are stronger than you.

112
00:06:47,924 --> 00:06:49,914
So the safest strategy is this.

113
00:06:50,756 --> 00:06:51,956
Don't make noise.

114
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:57,140
If another civilisation
discovers you, it might

115
00:06:57,220 --> 00:06:59,930
decide to destroy you
before you become a threat.

116
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:02,770
Therefore everyone stays silent.

117
00:07:03,614 --> 00:07:06,144
And that would neatly
explain the Fermi paradox.

118
00:07:06,774 --> 00:07:08,404
It's an elegant idea.

119
00:07:09,259 --> 00:07:12,469
But there's a problem.
A surprisingly big one.

120
00:07:13,039 --> 00:07:14,769
How exactly do you hide a planet?

121
00:07:17,899 --> 00:07:21,709
We don't broadcast deliberate
messages to the galaxy all the time.

122
00:07:22,539 --> 00:07:24,969
But Earth itself is extremely interesting.

123
00:07:25,890 --> 00:07:28,830
We have been modifying our
atmosphere, producing artificial

124
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:33,280
chemicals, and generating enormous
amounts of radio emission.

125
00:07:34,199 --> 00:07:38,249
And all of this is happening on a planet
that is already naturally observable.

126
00:07:39,074 --> 00:07:43,874
You don't have to detect an alien
radio message saying this. "Hello.

127
00:07:43,954 --> 00:07:45,154
We are humans."

128
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,650
You can look at the planet's atmosphere.

129
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:49,910
You can study its spectrum.

130
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:52,570
You can ask what gases are present.

131
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,540
A sufficiently advanced
telescope observing Earth as an

132
00:07:56,620 --> 00:08:00,010
exoplanet could detect the
chemicals in our atmosphere.

133
00:08:00,915 --> 00:08:04,885
Water vapour. Carbon
dioxide. Oxygen. Ozone.

134
00:08:05,834 --> 00:08:09,404
Of course, detecting oxygen does
not automatically mean this.

135
00:08:10,114 --> 00:08:11,504
"There are humans here."

136
00:08:12,204 --> 00:08:13,674
It could simply mean this.

137
00:08:14,144 --> 00:08:15,554
"This planet is interesting."

138
00:08:16,675 --> 00:08:19,885
But that's already enough to create
a problem for the Dark Forest.

139
00:08:20,655 --> 00:08:24,345
The argument doesn't require
them to know exactly what we are.

140
00:08:25,008 --> 00:08:29,358
It only requires them to notice that
something unusual is happening here.

141
00:08:30,032 --> 00:08:33,002
And Earth is not merely a rock
with a little bit of life.

142
00:08:33,672 --> 00:08:37,102
Its atmosphere has been
dramatically shaped by biology.

143
00:08:38,087 --> 00:08:39,287
And increasingly...

144
00:08:40,359 --> 00:08:41,559
by technology.

145
00:08:43,869 --> 00:08:47,359
And atmospheric chemistry
isn't even the only problem.

146
00:08:47,969 --> 00:08:52,599
Some of our strongest transmissions are
not intended for extraterrestrials at all.

147
00:08:53,333 --> 00:08:56,213
Powerful radar systems
can produce signals that

148
00:08:56,293 --> 00:08:59,503
are vastly stronger than
ordinary radio leakage.

149
00:09:00,383 --> 00:09:03,303
Researchers have investigated
how detectable Earth's

150
00:09:03,383 --> 00:09:06,653
technological activity
could be from other stars.

151
00:09:07,583 --> 00:09:10,643
Under favourable circumstances,
some of these signals

152
00:09:10,723 --> 00:09:13,053
could be detected across
thousands of light-years.

153
00:09:14,357 --> 00:09:18,487
That does not mean somebody could casually
look across the galaxy and say this.

154
00:09:18,877 --> 00:09:20,807
"There are humans on Earth."

155
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:23,570
That's not how astronomy works.

156
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:27,490
Detection depends on distance,
direction and sensitivity.

157
00:09:28,526 --> 00:09:30,756
But it does mean something
much more modest...

158
00:09:31,694 --> 00:09:33,084
and much more interesting.

159
00:09:34,222 --> 00:09:36,172
Earth is not perfectly hidden.

160
00:09:38,902 --> 00:09:41,352
We are already producing technosignatures.

161
00:09:41,502 --> 00:09:44,832
And we're not even trying
very hard to hide them.

162
00:09:45,507 --> 00:09:48,317
So does that kill the Dark
Forest? Not completely.

163
00:09:49,315 --> 00:09:53,825
A sufficiently advanced civilisation could
perhaps hide much better than we can.

164
00:09:54,775 --> 00:09:57,105
But now the Dark Forest has a new problem.

165
00:09:57,925 --> 00:10:00,675
It isn't enough for
civilisations to be afraid.

166
00:10:01,365 --> 00:10:04,095
They also have to be
extremely good at hiding.

167
00:10:04,945 --> 00:10:07,835
And every one of them has to reach
that conclusion independently.

168
00:10:09,075 --> 00:10:12,065
Because if even one
civilisation decides this.

169
00:10:12,841 --> 00:10:14,571
"We don't care. Let's expand."

170
00:10:15,582 --> 00:10:18,832
then the galaxy might eventually
become much less quiet.

171
00:10:19,667 --> 00:10:22,757
The Dark Forest therefore
doesn't solve the Fermi paradox.

172
00:10:23,567 --> 00:10:25,037
It adds another assumption.

173
00:10:25,887 --> 00:10:27,777
Almost everyone must be paranoid.

174
00:10:30,867 --> 00:10:33,777
We started with this.
"Where is everybody?"

175
00:10:34,827 --> 00:10:37,037
But perhaps that isn't the right question.

176
00:10:37,407 --> 00:10:39,737
Maybe the correct question is this.

177
00:10:40,578 --> 00:10:44,488
"What exactly should we expect an
alien civilisation to look like?"

178
00:10:45,175 --> 00:10:48,045
Do we expect it to expand? Why?

179
00:10:48,495 --> 00:10:52,705
Do we expect it to remain detectable
for millions of years? Why?

180
00:10:53,491 --> 00:10:54,861
And most importantly.

181
00:10:55,051 --> 00:10:58,341
Do we expect our
instruments to recognise it?

182
00:10:59,007 --> 00:11:01,497
We have one example of a
technological civilisation.

183
00:11:03,370 --> 00:11:04,570
One.

184
00:11:06,445 --> 00:11:09,235
And we've existed for
an absurdly short time.

185
00:11:10,105 --> 00:11:14,475
So perhaps we're making an enormous
extrapolation from a sample size of one.

186
00:11:17,183 --> 00:11:21,393
Perhaps somewhere in the galaxy,
somebody has already noticed our planet.

187
00:11:21,943 --> 00:11:24,053
Not because we sent them a message.

188
00:11:24,855 --> 00:11:26,055
But because...

189
00:11:26,849 --> 00:11:28,319
we are difficult to hide.

190
00:11:31,479 --> 00:11:32,889
So where does that leave us?

191
00:11:33,499 --> 00:11:37,049
Maybe intelligent life is
rare. Maybe it's common.

192
00:11:37,519 --> 00:11:40,909
Maybe they don't want to
expand. Maybe they're hiding.

193
00:11:41,738 --> 00:11:44,428
Every one of these
possibilities is conceivable.

194
00:11:45,258 --> 00:11:46,888
And that's precisely the problem.

195
00:11:47,767 --> 00:11:51,677
The Fermi paradox is presented as
if we knew one side of the equation.

196
00:11:52,727 --> 00:11:54,737
"The universe should be
full of civilisations."

197
00:11:55,907 --> 00:12:00,257
But we don't. So perhaps there is
simply a huge amount of uncertainty...

198
00:12:01,211 --> 00:12:03,541
wrapped inside a very catchy question.

199
00:12:04,357 --> 00:12:05,557
Where is everybody?

200
00:12:08,451 --> 00:12:11,411
Maybe someone is looking at
Earth right now and wondering

201
00:12:11,491 --> 00:12:14,881
what the hell is happening on
that strange little blue planet.

202
00:12:15,672 --> 00:12:18,562
And perhaps the most
important question isn't this.

203
00:12:19,373 --> 00:12:21,003
"Why haven't we found aliens?"

204
00:12:22,051 --> 00:12:23,621
It's this. "If they exist...

205
00:12:24,371 --> 00:12:26,521
would we even recognise
them when we see them?"
